    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1015">

                <pb facs="1016" n="1004" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg1004"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114A O.T./9–2144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-09-21T22:00:00+01:00">September 21,
                            1944—10 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 22—8:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6274. American Interests—Indochina. Legation’s 5069, August 7.<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d1015-f1"/> Note September 19 from Foreign Office states Swiss
                    Consulate, Saigon, reports unofficially advised that 1861 POWs detained Saigon
                    camp include 209 Americans. Camp leader is Captain J. H. Fowler. According a
                    detained officer, treatment satisfactory. Japanese Commanding Officer
                    correct.</p>
                <p>About 430 patients in hospital where dangerous and cruel soldier in charge.
                    Insufficient supplies given doctors who lack anti-tetanous serum, morphine,
                    pills and syringes.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
